Here is a Java program that implements a stack using a linked list:

```java
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

public class LinkedStack<T> {
    private Node<T> top;
    private int size;

    // Node class for creating nodes
    private static class Node<T> {
        private T data;
        private Node<T> next;

        public Node(T data) {
            this.data = data;
        }
    }

    // Push operation to add an element to the stack
    public void push(T element) {
        if (size >= 100) {
            pop();
        }
        Node<T> newNode = new Node<>(element);
        newNode.next = top;
        top = newNode;
        size++;
    }

    // Pop operation to remove and return the top element from the stack
    public T pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack is empty");
        }
        T data = top.data;
        top = top.next;
        size--;
        return data;
    }

    // Peek operation to return the top element without removing it from the stack
    public T peek() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return top.data;
    }

    // Check if the stack is empty
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }

    // Get the number of elements in the stack
    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    // Remove all elements from the stack
    public void clear() {
        top = null;
        size = 0;
    }

    // Convert the stack to an array
    public Object[] toArray() {
        Object[] arr = new Object[size];
        Node<T> current = top;
        int index = 0;
        while (current != null) {
            arr[index++] = current.data;
            current = current.next;
        }
        return arr;
    }

    // Check if the stack contains the specified element
    public boolean contains(T element) {
        Node<T> current = top;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.data.equals(element)) {
                return true;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        return false;
    }

    // Get the index of the first occurrence of the specified element in the stack
    public int indexOf(T element) {
        Node<T> current = top;
        int index = 0;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.data.equals(element)) {
                return index;
            }
            current = current.next;
            index++;
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
```

Now you can use this `LinkedStack` class to create a stack and perform various operations on it:

