You can implement a bubble sort algorithm in Java using the following code:

```java
public static void bubbleSort(int[] array) {  
    int n = array.length;
    int temp = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) {
            if (array[j] > array[j+1]) {
                temp = array[j];
                array[j] = array[j+1];
                array[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code defines a static method `bubbleSort` that takes an integer array as input. The algorithm iterates over the array multiple times, comparing adjacent elements and swapping them if they are out of order. This process continues until the array is fully sorted.

In the outer loop, `i` represents the number of passes made through the array, and in each pass, the largest elements "bubble" to the end of the array. The inner loop, controlled by variable `j`, iterates from the start of the array to `n-i-1`, comparing `array[j]` with `array[j+1]` and swapping them if necessary. After each pass, the largest element is guaranteed to be at the end of the array.

Once the algorithm completes, the original array will be sorted in ascending order.

In a switch statement, the "break" keyword is used to exit the switch block and prevent the execution of subsequent cases. Without using the "break" keyword, the switch statement will fall through to the next case, continuing the execution until a "break" or the end of the switch block is encountered.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```java
int day = 2;
String dayOfWeek;

switch (day) {
    case 1:
        dayOfWeek = "Monday";
        break;
    case 2:
        dayOfWeek = "Tuesday";
        break;
    case 3:
        dayOfWeek = "Wednesday";
        break;
    case 4:
        dayOfWeek = "Thursday";
        break;
    case 5:
        dayOfWeek = "Friday";
        break;
    default:
        dayOfWeek = "Invalid day";
}

System.out.println(dayOfWeek);
```

In this example, if the value of `day` is 2, the output will be "Tuesday" because the corresponding case is executed, and then the "break" statement is encountered, exiting the switch block. If the "break" statements were not present, the switch statement would fall through to the next case, resulting in "TuesdayWednesdayThursdayFriday" being assigned to `dayOfWeek` and printed.

Using the "break" keyword is important to ensure that only the desired case is executed and to prevent unintended fall-through behavior. Without the "break" keyword, multiple cases may be executed, leading to incorrect or unexpected results.

