A common denominator is a number that can be divided by all the denominators of the fractions that we are adding. To find a common denominator, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the denominators.
Here is the Java code to find the common denominator and add the fractions:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class AddFractions {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
 System.out.print("Enter the first fraction numerator: ");
 int num1 = sc.nextInt();
 System.out.print("Enter the first fraction denominator: ");
 int den1 = sc.nextInt();
 System.out.print("Enter the second fraction numerator: ");
 int num2 = sc.nextInt();
 System.out.print("Enter the second fraction denominator: ");
 int den2 = sc.nextInt();
 int lcm = findLCM(den1, den2);
 int newNum1 = num1 * (lcm / den1);
 int newNum2 = num2 * (lcm / den2);
 int sumNum = newNum1 + newNum2;
 System.out.println("The sum of " + num1 + "/" + den1 + " and " + num2 + "/" + den2 + " is " + sumNum + "/" + lcm);
 }
 public static int findLCM(int a, int b) {
 int max = Math.max(a, b);
 int min = Math.min(a, b);
 int lcm = max;
 while (lcm % min != 0) {
 lcm += max;
 }
 return lcm;
 }
}
```
In this program, we first ask the user to enter the numerators and denominators of the two fractions. Then, we find the LCM of the denominators using the `findLCM()` method. We use this LCM to convert the fractions to equivalent fractions with the same denominator. Finally, we add the numerators and display the sum as a fraction with the common denominator.

